s largely canceled by a favorable Democratic environment in this open seat, and raters split between Tossup and slight Republican ratings. The lone recent poll shows James up one point, prediction markets price her narrowly favored, and money is roughly even, leaving a near-even race with a faint Democratic edge.", "watch": "A fresh independent poll or a shift in the national midterm environment could break the race clearly toward either candidate.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 66 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Ashley Hinson (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Ashley Hinson's retirement to run for Senate leaves an open Republican-leaning seat in eastern Iowa, where Republican Joe Mitchell faces Democrat Lindsay James. Most forecasters call the race a tossup, with a few tilting Republican and one tipping toward a Democratic flip. The only recent public survey, conducted in July for a Democratic sponsor, showed James up by a single point, and prediction markets price the contest nearly even with a slight Democratic edge.\n\nThe district's Republican lean is roughly offset by a national environment favoring Democrats, and neither candidate benefits from incumbency. Fundraising is close, with Mitchell slightly ahead in dollars raised, while Democratic outside groups have spent against him and his lobbying background has drawn scrutiny. With little recent polling, uncertainty is high, and small shifts in the environment could decide the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5595, "margin": 0.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5684, "margin": 0.7}]}